nd the molecule name itself. The underlying pattern seems to be \"deficiency-of-molecule\" constructions, where \"deficiency\" acts as a disease-class trigger word and the complement (like C7) is a recognized gene/protein name that together form a real disease name, but the exact span the model wraps around this construction doesn't match gold's boundary choice.\n\nThis points to a clear pattern: phrases headed by \"deficiency\" governing an \"of\"-PP naming a gene or protein get mislabeled as disease spans, when true disease annotations are proper names or acronyms like \"Coats disease\" or \"SLS\" rather than descriptive molecular-deficit phrases. The true positives confirm this—cases like \"a deficiency of norrin\" or \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase\" aren't annotated as diseases, while the actual disease name in that same sentence is something distinct like \"Sjogren-Larsson syndrome.\"\n\nSo the rule I'm forming is that \"deficiency\" only counts as a disease head when it's a lexicalized compound like \"C7 deficiency\" with the molecule as a premodifier, not when it's part of an \"of\"-PP construction describing a phenotypic state. In those PP cases, I should either skip labeling or truncate to the minimal compound form, excluding modifiers like \"complete\" or \"heterozygous\" from the span—though I'm second-guessing whether \"deficiency of C7\" might still be gold-labeled, so I need to be cautious about overgeneralizing this rule.\n\nThe key contrastive evidence is that the same \"deficiency of [molecule]\" construction appears elsewhere in the true-positive set without being annotated—like \"a deficiency of norrin\"—which strengthens the case that it's the descriptive biochemical clause versus a named disease that drives the distinction. The distinguishing boolean feature comes down to whether the mention is a lexicalized disease name versus a descriptive clause naming a biochemical state.\n\nSo the trigger is \"deficiency\" followed by 