onent of complement.\"\n\n**Hypothesis check.**\n- The trigger explains both examples.\n- The true positives contain the same trigger construction without a disease label. \"results in a deficiency of norrin\" (10484772) and \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase\" (10577908) are not annotated as diseases. This is the strongest control evidence: the same head-plus-*of* structure is not a disease in the gold standard.\n- Caveat: gold and LLM both carry the label SpecificDisease. So the error is most likely in span extent (the LLM span includes the \"deficiency of X\" clause), not in the class decision. The gold span should be verified before applying the rule.\n\n## Step 2: Pattern Formulation\n\n- **Pattern Insight: Generic \"deficiency of [molecule]\" phrases over-extended as SpecificDisease spans**\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** The state noun \"deficiency\" heads an *of*-PP that names a complement or gene-product component (C7, \"the seventh component of complement\"). The model treats the whole NP as a disease name, which extends the span across the preposition and includes the molecular argument.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** In 10484772, \"a deficiency of norrin,\" and in 10577908, \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase,\" use the same head-plus-*of*-plus-molecule construction and are not annotated as diseases. The true disease mentions are lexicalized names whose spans are the names themselves (Coats disease, Norrie disease, SLS), with no *of*-argument.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):**\n    1. A SpecificDisease span must be the minimal lexicalized name. Do not extend it across \"of\" to include a molecular or component argument.\n    2. Treat \"deficiency\" as part of a disease name only in the premodifier-head compound form (e.g., \"C7 deficiency\"), where the molecule modifies a fixed head and the whole is a recognized disease name. Treat \"deficiency of [molecule]\" as a descriptive state, not a disease span, and trim the span